2   1   A description of the requirements for clergy.
2   2   In those times everyone could be married, and most were, although this was never obligatory.
2   3   Women are mentioned as deaconesses, who also existed in those days.
2   4   Today this institution has been preserved in the Patriarchate of Alexandria, and there are also views that it ought to be restored more broadly.
2   5   The list of requirements is fairly clear; primarily it concerns tested and experienced candidates who also hold a good reputation among the gentiles, since the majority of people surrounding them at that time were gentiles (Timothy, like Paul, laboured predominantly in a gentile environment).
2   6   Many of these requirements became part of canon law with certain modifications (in particular, bishops today, and for many centuries now, are exclusively unmarried).
