1   1   Here is read the parable of the steward of unjustness and the beginning of Jesus&#39; explanation of it.
1   2   It is a difficult parable to understand.
1   3   Yet it can be grasped if one pays attention to Jesus&#39; explanation — the steward, by forgiving a portion of the debts owed by his master&#39;s debtors, showed mercy to them.
1   4   Christ desires that the faithful show mercy toward other sinful people, so that sin may become less.
1   5   And the very title steward of unrighteousness signifies that everything good and worthy that a person possesses is not their own by origin, but has its beginning in God.
1   6   It is precisely God who gives a person particular gifts, and only then does the person receive something genuinely good, possessing something genuinely good.
1   7   In such a case, every person stands like that steward — managing, yet not what belongs properly to themselves, not what is exclusively their own.
1   8   Therefore, they manage not their own wealth, but what God gives them.
1   9   And since this wealth is originally God&#39;s, a manifestation of divine generosity, they hold it as it were unrighteously — that is, undeservedly, as a free gift from God.
1   10   This is a state of becoming, when a person has not yet become who they will be, when the Kingdom of God is not yet manifest and final.
1   11   Yet when the Kingdom of God comes, after the Second Coming of Christ and the general resurrection of the dead, then a person will cease to be a steward, and will become what they have become through God&#39;s mercy and their own freedom.
1   12   Why then is stewardship taken away completely?
